# Line endings are LF, in the repository and in every working tree.
#
# The files under src/help are compiled into the binary with include_str!, so
# whatever bytes a checkout produces are the bytes --help prints and the
# manual page is rendered from. On Windows, git checks text out with CRLF
# unless something says otherwise, and something has to be this file.
#
# What that cost, before it was written: a blank line inside an option's long
# help arrived as ten spaces and a carriage return, the trim that takes
# trailing padding off stopped at the return and left all ten, and --help came
# out with 59 lines of whitespace on windows-latest where Linux and macOS had
# none. The trim was taught about carriage returns, because a checkout is not
# the only way one arrives. This is the other half of it: the corpus now reads
# the same everywhere, so --help and the page are the same bytes on every
# platform rather than the same bytes wherever they happened to be built.
*                       text=auto eol=lf

# A fixture that is *about* line endings would have to keep the bytes it was
# written with, and the rule above would take them away.
#
# There is none today. Every CRLF case in the tests and the checks is built in
# code -- tests/cli.rs, src/main.rs, and three of the checks all write \r\n
# themselves -- which is where a fixture about line endings belongs, because
# no checkout can reach it there. If one is ever written to disk instead,
# exempt that path here:
#
#   testdata/crlf.csv   -text
#
# rather than weakening the rule above for everything else.
